<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-stats" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Statistical Thinking</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        <em> There are three kinds of lies: lies, damned lies, and statistics. </em>
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The true origin of this quote is debateable<fn><url href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lies,_damned_lies,_and_statistics"/></fn>, but it is the warning that is important for us.
      Quantitative information can be tricky to understand, and we can all be tricked!
      We saw this in <xref ref="ch-prop-reasoning"/> when we could get different numerical answers depending on the base of the percentage.
      That doesn't mean the answer is wrong, it just means that we need to be careful with the words we use to describe what the quantity represents.
    </p>
    <p>
      The words will be just as important when learning some statistics.
      There are plenty of mathematical formulas in this field of study; many quite complex!
      Our focus here will be not be on using these formulas, computing our own statistics, designing our own studies, or making our own predictions.
      We're focused here on becoming knowledgable consumers of data. We don't want to be tricked!
    </p>
    <p>
      Statistical methods arise in every field and in many parts of our regular lives.
      What does this cancer treatment study really tell me about my risks?
      What does this report about local school performance tell me about how well my kid is reading?
      Is this politician telling the <alert>whole</alert> truth in this campaign speech?
    </p>
   </introduction>

